00:48:35.210 --> 00:48:42.459
Michael Kantor: inside ecosystems like Bitcoin, through ordinals and ruins, and also other, networks like,

300
00:48:43.370 --> 00:48:52.199
Michael Kantor: you've got other on-chain networks, like Doge, for example, that have adopted these on-chain specifications for storing content, and so forth.

301
00:48:52.430 --> 00:49:08.780
Michael Kantor: So, essentially, they are on-chain patterns, application-level patterns and implementations for things like file storage, data registration, profile management, communication, and facilitation. Good examples of that are HCS1, HCS2, and 10.

302
00:49:09.180 --> 00:49:20.599
Michael Kantor: which all enable these sorts of functionalities. They are, at its core, very much based on top of JSON schemas, so you have these predefined schemas to do certain things.

