313
00:51:06.180 --> 00:51:17.240
Michael Kantor: how you actually use these standards, and how you can conceptualize it. So, a good example of this is HCS1, and this was actually a similar slide that I had done during Hedericon.

314
00:51:17.360 --> 00:51:27.480
Michael Kantor: So for example, at HCS1, it enables you to store and retrieve files on Graph using the Hedera Consensus Service.

315
00:51:27.730 --> 00:51:31.949
Michael Kantor: So imagine you have a 4KB JPEG,

316
00:51:32.060 --> 00:51:45.879
Michael Kantor: what we do with that, JPEG, essentially, first, we create a new topic on the Hedera Consensus Service, and then we take that JPEG and we break it up into four little pieces, because you can only put one kilobyte per message in the Hedera Consensus Service.

317
00:51:45.880 --> 00:51:57.380
Michael Kantor: So we take those four pieces, and we write them as messages into that topic ID, and then we compress those messages before we store them using an algorithm like ZSTD,

318
00:51:57.680 --> 00:52:17.239
Michael Kantor: For example, so we can get the size down a little bit. And then inside, places like wallets, for example, Hashpack, they will, be able to call that topic ID, pull in those pieces, stitch them back together, and then you have that JPEG. But the actual contents of the file are stored through the consensus service itself.

319
00:52:18.730 --> 00:52:32.110
Michael Kantor: So inside of each standard, we have these specified message structures, and every standard is different, but they do share similar field names in many places, that you can look back to.

320
00:52:32.110 --> 00:52:41.649
Michael Kantor: HCS1 was one of our first standards, and the way it works is each message has two primary fields inside of it.

321
00:52:41.940 --> 00:52:50.259
Michael Kantor: that you refer back to. There is an O field that orders the messages, and there's a C field that has the content

322
00:52:50.260 --> 00:53:09.959
Michael Kantor: of the actual message. So, each C inside of the message, there could be hundreds of these, is the actual compressed 1KB chunk, that's put into that message. It's a little bit less than kilobyte, so that we can fit into the actual message. And then we have the O field.

323
00:53:10.370 --> 00:53:23.210
Michael Kantor: which orders it. And the O field is really important inside of HCS1 because it means that you can create a 100 kilobyte image and submit all the transactions at the same time.

324
00:53:23.220 --> 00:53:40.910
Michael Kantor: Because you don't have to do it sequentially, like 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and wait for each transaction to settle. You do them all at the same time in parallel if you want, and then you have the O field, which you can use to sort the actual file contents later on when you want to stitch back the image.

325
00:53:40.910 --> 00:53:44.239
Michael Kantor: So it's great at scale, and actually,

326
00:53:44.490 --> 00:54:03.759
